From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hotty Agent <hotty@neuroplast.io>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 00:50:57 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 42/44] gtk: paint a new frame now, not a refresh after the
 last input

GTK handles every input event in a frame clock cycle of its own
(gdk_surface_handle_event requests the flush-events phase). That
cycle paints nothing, so GDK's idle frame clock holds the next cycle
back until a refresh after it began (min_next_frame_time), to keep a
clock that isn't synced to vblank from busy looping. A key's frame is
ready a few milliseconds after the key, and then waits out the rest of
that refresh before GTK paints it: every keystroke shows a frame later
than it could.

When the renderer hands GTK a frame, the surface now queues its draw
and, if the frame clock's source is waiting only for that hold, makes
it ready now. The pace stays the compositor's: after a paint, GDK
freezes the surface's updates until the frame callback, and the
clock, stopped, has no source, so a frame drawn meanwhile waits for
the callback as before.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/apprt/gtk/class/render_surface.zig | 20 ++++++++++++++++++++
 src/apprt/gtk/class/surface.zig        |  2 +-
 2 files changed, 21 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/apprt/gtk/class/render_surface.zig b/src/apprt/gtk/class/render_surface.zig
index d98dccd06..df5b520ee 100644
--- a/src/apprt/gtk/class/render_surface.zig
+++ b/src/apprt/gtk/class/render_surface.zig
@@ -251,6 +251,26 @@ pub const RenderSurface = extern struct {
         }
     }
 
+    /// hottyterm: asks GTK to paint the renderer's new frame, now rather
+    /// than a refresh late. GTK handles every input event in a frame clock
+    /// cycle of its own, and a cycle that paints nothing holds the next one
+    /// back until a refresh after it began (`min_next_frame_time` in GDK's
+    /// gdkframeclockidle.c), so a key's frame, ready a few ms after the
+    /// key, would wait out the rest of that refresh. When GTK's clock is
+    /// waiting only for that, its source runs now. The pace stays the
+    /// compositor's: after a paint, GDK freezes the surface's updates
+    /// until the frame callback (gdksurface-wayland.c), and the clock,
+    /// stopped, has no source, so nothing changes until the callback.
+    pub fn queueFrame(self: *Self) void {
+        const widget = self.as(gtk.Widget);
+        widget.queueDraw();
+        const clock = widget.getFrameClock() orelse return;
+        const source = glib.MainContext.findSourceByUserData(null, clock) orelse return;
+        const name = source.getName() orelse return;
+        if (!std.mem.eql(u8, std.mem.span(name), "[gtk] gdk_frame_clock_frame")) return;
+        source.setReadyTime(0);
+    }
+
     //---------------------------------------------------------------
     // Presenting
 
diff --git a/src/apprt/gtk/class/surface.zig b/src/apprt/gtk/class/surface.zig
index fd2a39dfd..86c3eab74 100644
--- a/src/apprt/gtk/class/surface.zig
+++ b/src/apprt/gtk/class/surface.zig
@@ -849,7 +849,7 @@ pub const Surface = extern struct {
     pub fn redraw(self: *Self) void {
         const priv = self.private();
         hotty.trace.instant("term.redraw"); // hottyterm: gov R-22's stamps
-        priv.render_surface.as(gtk.Widget).queueDraw();
+        priv.render_surface.queueFrame(); // hottyterm: now, not a refresh late
     }
 
     /// Callback used to determine whether border should be shown around the
